#!/command/with-contenv sh
# with-contenv: POSTGRES_USER/POSTGRES_PASSWORD/POSTGRES_DB (set in
# deployments/compose/metrics-collector.yml) have to be pushed back into
# this longrun's own environment - S6_KEEP_ENV is left at its default (0),
# same convention documented in ../metrics-collector/run and
# Database-Vault's own postgres longrun.
#
# docker-entrypoint.sh is the upstream timescale/timescaledb image's own
# entrypoint (Postgres-based, same contract as Database-Vault's own
# postgres:17 base) - execing it, not reimplementing initdb, keeps
# first-run bootstrap and the POSTGRES_USER/POSTGRES_PASSWORD/POSTGRES_DB-
# driven behavior identical to upstream's own documented contract.
#
# Unlike Database-Vault's own Postgres ("-c listen_addresses=localhost",
# reachable from nothing but that container's own Go binary), TimescaleDB
# here is bound to every LOCAL interface ("-c listen_addresses=*") on
# purpose: Grafana's own outbound query connection (MT-F-04, UC-05) is a
# direct Postgres-wire connection from ANOTHER Proxmox guest, not a call
# routed through Metrics-Collector's own Go process - the mesh interface
# this node's tailscaled longrun creates (see ../tailscaled/run) is one of
# the "local interfaces" this flag covers, so binding to "*" (rather than
# a single, dynamically-assigned mesh IP this longrun has no ordering
# dependency on) is what makes TimescaleDB reachable from Grafana's guest
# at all. In production (no ramusb-net, no host-published port - see this
# image's Dockerfile, "No EXPOSE") the ONLY interfaces that exist on this
# guest are loopback and the mesh, so "*" is not a general network
# exposure there. In THIS project's dev Compose stack, this container also
# stays attached to ramusb-net (for CA-token minting/MQTT reachability,
# same as every other real-tailscaled service here) - meaning TimescaleDB
# is also reachable from any other ramusb-net container in dev, the same
# deferred, dev-only, cosmetic dual-reachability limitation already
# accepted for Certificate-Authority/MQTT-broker (docs/Known_Issues.md's
# KI-05) - authentication still requires BOTH the real POSTGRES_PASSWORD
# AND a valid mTLS client certificate for the connecting role either way
# (KI-22, RNF-SEC-04): third-party/timescaledb/pg_hba-mtls.sh REPLACES the
# upstream image's own default, plaintext-permissive pg_hba.conf rule with
# a `hostssl ... clientcert=verify-full` one before TimescaleDB ever
# accepts its first network connection.
#
# ssl_cert_file/ssl_key_file/ssl_ca_file (KI-22, RNF-SEC-04): TimescaleDB's
# own server identity, minted/kept renewed by
# metrics-collector-timescaledb-cert-issuer/-renewer
# (deployments/compose/metrics-collector.yml) onto the
# ramusb-metrics-collector-timescaledb-certs named volume, mounted
# read-only here at /run/timescaledb-certs - same "sidecar mints onto a
# shared volume, this longrun only ever reads from it" shape as
# Mosquitto's own KI-16 design, adapted for a co-located rather than
# standalone third-party process. The renewer
# (metrics-collector-timescaledb-cert-renewer,
# deployments/compose/metrics-collector.yml) shares this container's PID
# namespace (`pid: "service:metrics-collector"`) and sends a direct
# SIGHUP to the real postmaster PID once new certs land on the shared
# volume - the same reload mechanism `pg_ctl reload`/`SELECT
# pg_reload_conf()` trigger internally, just invoked directly since the
# renewer is an external sidecar, not a same-process SQL client.
# ssl_ca_file is REQUIRED for
# clientcert=verify-full (pg_hba-mtls.conf, see
# third-party/timescaledb/pg_hba-mtls.sh) to have a root to verify
# Grafana's own client certificate against - without it Postgres refuses
# to start with SSL client-cert verification requested at all.
#
# exec replaces this shell so docker-entrypoint.sh's own re-exec chain
# (gosu to the "postgres" system user, then finally the real postgres
# binary) becomes the directly supervised process tree and receives s6's
# signals itself.
exec /usr/local/bin/docker-entrypoint.sh postgres -c listen_addresses=* \
	-c ssl=on \
	-c ssl_cert_file=/run/timescaledb-certs/timescaledb.dev-only.crt \
	-c ssl_key_file=/run/timescaledb-certs/timescaledb.dev-only.key \
	-c ssl_ca_file=/run/timescaledb-certs/timescaledb-dev-ca.dev-only.crt
